{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MmWKSwqwHLKK4drztSoPsA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017QtP7KP4Av3Dt5Ds1KpGxR","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Systematic Comparison of Classification, Generation, and Trajectory Approaches\"\n\nThe paper compares three formulations for neural code verification (predicting pass/fail without execution): Classification (CLS, predict single token), Constrained Word Model (CWM, generate execution output then match), and Trajectory (TRAJ, predict overall resolution status). Comparison spans 3 formulations × 2 model families (Qwen3 4B/8B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B) × 2 complexity levels (function-level, repo-level), trained on SWE-bench-PerTest (489 instances, 9784 test samples).\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Error Complementarity as Verification Strategy: CLS and CWM have different failure modes (κ=0.24–0.31), oracle ensembles lift +2–3pp.\n2. First Systematic Formulation Comparison: Formulation effectiveness is model-contingent (no universal winner), interaction p=0.03.\n3. Efficiency–Accuracy Tradeoff: CLS offers 6–9× speedup when accuracy is equivalent.\n\n## Key Tables (from paper)\n\n**Table 2: Full accuracy matrix**\n| Formulation | Qwen Func-4B | Qwen Func-8B | Qwen Repo-4B | Qwen Repo-8B | DS Func | DS Repo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CLS | 87.83 | 90.48 | 82.35 | 84.79 | 86.85 | 63.5 |\n| CWM | 87.51 | 87.67 | 58.04† | — | 86.83 | 65.6 |\n| TRAJ | 85.66 | 87.31 | — | — | 84.7 | 59.3 |\n† 93% unparseable. — = not completed.\n\n**Table 3: CWM unparseable rates**\nQwen-4B tok64: 93%, Qwen-4B tok1024: 53.7%, DeepSeek: 11.8%\n\n**Table 4: Regression for CLS-CWM gap**\nModel family is only significant predictor (p=0.03, R²=0.08). Complexity proxies not significant.\n\n**Table 5: Error complementarity**\nQwen-8B: κ=0.24, oracle=90.63%, lift=+3.12pp\nDeepSeek: κ=0.31, oracle=89.2%, lift=+2.35pp\n\n**Table 6: Error taxonomy** (200 annotated samples, inter-annotator κ=0.72)\nCLS errors: logic misunderstanding 38%, context truncation 22%, label noise 15%, edge case miss 14%, spurious correlation 11%\nCWM errors: output format mismatch 31%, numeric precision 24%, string encoding 18%, execution path error 16%, hallucinated output 11%\n\n**Table 7: Efficiency**\nCLS speedup: 6.3–8.9× over CWM. Batch: CLS 64, CWM 8.\n\n**Table 8: DeepSeek func-level 3-formulation (2-seed)**\nCLS 86.85% (std 0.11), CWM 86.83% (std 1.49), TRAJ 84.7% (std 1.20)\n\n**Table 9: Re-repair experiment (200 instances)**\nControl: 17.5%, CLS diagnostic: 18.0% (p=0.72), Oracle: 21.5% (p=0.11), Random: 14.5% (p=0.04*)\n\n**Table 4 regression**: All complexity predictors non-significant (p>0.18); model family p=0.03, R²=0.08.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan included these experiments:\n1. SWE-bench-PerTest Dataset Construction (489 instances, infrastructure)\n2. DeepSeek Repo-Level CLS Training (predicted acc 63.5%, below majority baseline 72.96%)\n3. DeepSeek Repo-Level CWM Training (predicted acc 65.6%, unparseable 11.8%)\n4. DeepSeek Repo-Level Trajectory Training (predicted acc 59.3%)\n5. DeepSeek Repo-Level Trajectory Seed123 (acc 61.2%, seed gap 1.9pp)\n6. DeepSeek Func-Level Trajectory corrected evaluation (mean acc 84.7%)\n7. Diagnostic Re-Repair Experiment (200 instances, negative result: CLS +0.5pp p=0.72)\n8. Cross-Model Error Complementarity Analysis (oracle ensemble 89.2% DeepSeek, 90.63% Qwen)\n9. Efficiency Comparison CLS vs CWM (6–9× speedup)\n10. Systematic 3×2×2 Formulation Comparison Table\n11. Error Taxonomy & Failure Mode Qualitative Analysis (200 samples)\n12. Complexity Proxy Regression Analysis (model family only significant predictor)\n13. Reviewer Critical Response Assessment: 2/4 reviewer criticals addressed. Critical 3 (no downstream impact) NOT addressed, Critical 4 (cross-model validation) NOT addressed — DeepSeek shows OPPOSITE pattern to Qwen.\n\nKey context: The paper's original framing was \"CLS scales better with complexity\" but cross-model evidence contradicts this. The paper has been reframed as \"formulation design space characterization.\" DeepSeek repo-level results are ALL below majority baseline (63.5%, 65.6%, 59.3% vs 72.96%), meaning the models are essentially not learning effectively at repo-level for DeepSeek.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the experimental controls adequate? Is the comparison fair? Are there confounds?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are claims properly supported? Significance testing? Seed variance?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: How complete is the design matrix? Do missing cells weaken claims?\n4. **Result interpretation**: Are conclusions well-supported? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to reproduce?\n\nFormat your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: Major/Minor)\n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) and Confidence (1-5)\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29352,"cache_read_input_tokens":23690,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":29352},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dfc6633b-34f1-47e9-a9c0-c3551e8f2a09","uuid":"f168924c-59b1-4642-9627-fccc3fe6acb0"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a1a292cbff6b51daa","tool_use_id":"toolu_017QtP7KP4Av3Dt5Ds1KpGxR","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Systematic Comparison of Classification, Generation, and Trajectory Approaches\"\n\nThe paper compares three formulations for neural code verification (predicting pass/fail without execution): Classification (CLS, predict single token), Constrained Word Model (CWM, generate execution output then match), and Trajectory (TRAJ, predict overall resolution status). Comparison spans 3 formulations × 2 model families (Qwen3 4B/8B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B) × 2 complexity levels (function-level, repo-level), trained on SWE-bench-PerTest (489 instances, 9784 test samples).\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Error Complementarity as Verification Strategy: CLS and CWM have different failure modes (κ=0.24–0.31), oracle ensembles lift +2–3pp.\n2. First Systematic Formulation Comparison: Formulation effectiveness is model-contingent (no universal winner), interaction p=0.03.\n3. Efficiency–Accuracy Tradeoff: CLS offers 6–9× speedup when accuracy is equivalent.\n\n## Key Tables (from paper)\n\n**Table 2: Full accuracy matrix**\n| Formulation | Qwen Func-4B | Qwen Func-8B | Qwen Repo-4B | Qwen Repo-8B | DS Func | DS Repo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CLS | 87.83 | 90.48 | 82.35 | 84.79 | 86.85 | 63.5 |\n| CWM | 87.51 | 87.67 | 58.04† | — | 86.83 | 65.6 |\n| TRAJ | 85.66 | 87.31 | — | — | 84.7 | 59.3 |\n† 93% unparseable. — = not completed.\n\n**Table 3: CWM unparseable rates**\nQwen-4B tok64: 93%, Qwen-4B tok1024: 53.7%, DeepSeek: 11.8%\n\n**Table 4: Regression for CLS-CWM gap**\nModel family is only significant predictor (p=0.03, R²=0.08). Complexity proxies not significant.\n\n**Table 5: Error complementarity**\nQwen-8B: κ=0.24, oracle=90.63%, lift=+3.12pp\nDeepSeek: κ=0.31, oracle=89.2%, lift=+2.35pp\n\n**Table 6: Error taxonomy** (200 annotated samples, inter-annotator κ=0.72)\nCLS errors: logic misunderstanding 38%, context truncation 22%, label noise 15%, edge case miss 14%, spurious correlation 11%\nCWM errors: output format mismatch 31%, numeric precision 24%, string encoding 18%, execution path error 16%, hallucinated output 11%\n\n**Table 7: Efficiency**\nCLS speedup: 6.3–8.9× over CWM. Batch: CLS 64, CWM 8.\n\n**Table 8: DeepSeek func-level 3-formulation (2-seed)**\nCLS 86.85% (std 0.11), CWM 86.83% (std 1.49), TRAJ 84.7% (std 1.20)\n\n**Table 9: Re-repair experiment (200 instances)**\nControl: 17.5%, CLS diagnostic: 18.0% (p=0.72), Oracle: 21.5% (p=0.11), Random: 14.5% (p=0.04*)\n\n**Table 4 regression**: All complexity predictors non-significant (p>0.18); model family p=0.03, R²=0.08.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan included these experiments:\n1. SWE-bench-PerTest Dataset Construction (489 instances, infrastructure)\n2. DeepSeek Repo-Level CLS Training (predicted acc 63.5%, below majority baseline 72.96%)\n3. DeepSeek Repo-Level CWM Training (predicted acc 65.6%, unparseable 11.8%)\n4. DeepSeek Repo-Level Trajectory Training (predicted acc 59.3%)\n5. DeepSeek Repo-Level Trajectory Seed123 (acc 61.2%, seed gap 1.9pp)\n6. DeepSeek Func-Level Trajectory corrected evaluation (mean acc 84.7%)\n7. Diagnostic Re-Repair Experiment (200 instances, negative result: CLS +0.5pp p=0.72)\n8. Cross-Model Error Complementarity Analysis (oracle ensemble 89.2% DeepSeek, 90.63% Qwen)\n9. Efficiency Comparison CLS vs CWM (6–9× speedup)\n10. Systematic 3×2×2 Formulation Comparison Table\n11. Error Taxonomy & Failure Mode Qualitative Analysis (200 samples)\n12. Complexity Proxy Regression Analysis (model family only significant predictor)\n13. Reviewer Critical Response Assessment: 2/4 reviewer criticals addressed. Critical 3 (no downstream impact) NOT addressed, Critical 4 (cross-model validation) NOT addressed — DeepSeek shows OPPOSITE pattern to Qwen.\n\nKey context: The paper's original framing was \"CLS scales better with complexity\" but cross-model evidence contradicts this. The paper has been reframed as \"formulation design space characterization.\" DeepSeek repo-level results are ALL below majority baseline (63.5%, 65.6%, 59.3% vs 72.96%), meaning the models are essentially not learning effectively at repo-level for DeepSeek.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the experimental controls adequate? Is the comparison fair? Are there confounds?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are claims properly supported? Significance testing? Seed variance?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: How complete is the design matrix? Do missing cells weaken claims?\n4. **Result interpretation**: Are conclusions well-supported? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to reproduce?\n\nFormat your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: Major/Minor)\n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) and Confidence (1-5)\n","uuid":"25fc1cc3-bcef-437d-bc1c-dd0a7687243d","session_id":"dfc6633b-34f1-47e9-a9c0-c3551e8f2a09"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"The Formulation Design Space for Neural Code Verification: A Systematic Comparison of Classification, Generation, and Trajectory Approaches\"\n\nThe paper compares three formulations for neural code verification (predicting pass/fail without execution): Classification (CLS, predict single token), Constrained Word Model (CWM, generate execution output then match), and Trajectory (TRAJ, predict overall resolution status). Comparison spans 3 formulations × 2 model families (Qwen3 4B/8B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B) × 2 complexity levels (function-level, repo-level), trained on SWE-bench-PerTest (489 instances, 9784 test samples).\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Error Complementarity as Verification Strategy: CLS and CWM have different failure modes (κ=0.24–0.31), oracle ensembles lift +2–3pp.\n2. First Systematic Formulation Comparison: Formulation effectiveness is model-contingent (no universal winner), interaction p=0.03.\n3. Efficiency–Accuracy Tradeoff: CLS offers 6–9× speedup when accuracy is equivalent.\n\n## Key Tables (from paper)\n\n**Table 2: Full accuracy matrix**\n| Formulation | Qwen Func-4B | Qwen Func-8B | Qwen Repo-4B | Qwen Repo-8B | DS Func | DS Repo |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CLS | 87.83 | 90.48 | 82.35 | 84.79 | 86.85 | 63.5 |\n| CWM | 87.51 | 87.67 | 58.04† | — | 86.83 | 65.6 |\n| TRAJ | 85.66 | 87.31 | — | — | 84.7 | 59.3 |\n† 93% unparseable. — = not completed.\n\n**Table 3: CWM unparseable rates**\nQwen-4B tok64: 93%, Qwen-4B tok1024: 53.7%, DeepSeek: 11.8%\n\n**Table 4: Regression for CLS-CWM gap**\nModel family is only significant predictor (p=0.03, R²=0.08). Complexity proxies not significant.\n\n**Table 5: Error complementarity**\nQwen-8B: κ=0.24, oracle=90.63%, lift=+3.12pp\nDeepSeek: κ=0.31, oracle=89.2%, lift=+2.35pp\n\n**Table 6: Error taxonomy** (200 annotated samples, inter-annotator κ=0.72)\nCLS errors: logic misunderstanding 38%, context truncation 22%, label noise 15%, edge case miss 14%, spurious correlation 11%\nCWM errors: output format mismatch 31%, numeric precision 24%, string encoding 18%, execution path error 16%, hallucinated output 11%\n\n**Table 7: Efficiency**\nCLS speedup: 6.3–8.9× over CWM. Batch: CLS 64, CWM 8.\n\n**Table 8: DeepSeek func-level 3-formulation (2-seed)**\nCLS 86.85% (std 0.11), CWM 86.83% (std 1.49), TRAJ 84.7% (std 1.20)\n\n**Table 9: Re-repair experiment (200 instances)**\nControl: 17.5%, CLS diagnostic: 18.0% (p=0.72), Oracle: 21.5% (p=0.11), Random: 14.5% (p=0.04*)\n\n**Table 4 regression**: All complexity predictors non-significant (p>0.18); model family p=0.03, R²=0.08.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\nThe full experimental plan included these experiments:\n1. SWE-bench-PerTest Dataset Construction (489 instances, infrastructure)\n2. DeepSeek Repo-Level CLS Training (predicted acc 63.5%, below majority baseline 72.96%)\n3. DeepSeek Repo-Level CWM Training (predicted acc 65.6%, unparseable 11.8%)\n4. DeepSeek Repo-Level Trajectory Training (predicted acc 59.3%)\n5. DeepSeek Repo-Level Trajectory Seed123 (acc 61.2%, seed gap 1.9pp)\n6. DeepSeek Func-Level Trajectory corrected evaluation (mean acc 84.7%)\n7. Diagnostic Re-Repair Experiment (200 instances, negative result: CLS +0.5pp p=0.72)\n8. Cross-Model Error Complementarity Analysis (oracle ensemble 89.2% DeepSeek, 90.63% Qwen)\n9. Efficiency Comparison CLS vs CWM (6–9× speedup)\n10. Systematic 3×2×2 Formulation Comparison Table\n11. Error Taxonomy & Failure Mode Qualitative Analysis (200 samples)\n12. Complexity Proxy Regression Analysis (model family only significant predictor)\n13. Reviewer Critical Response Assessment: 2/4 reviewer criticals addressed. Critical 3 (no downstream impact) NOT addressed, Critical 4 (cross-model validation) NOT addressed — DeepSeek shows OPPOSITE pattern to Qwen.\n\nKey context: The paper's original framing was \"CLS scales better with complexity\" but cross-model evidence contradicts this. The paper has been reframed as \"formulation design space characterization.\" DeepSeek repo-level results are ALL below majority baseline (63.5%, 65.6%, 59.3% vs 72.96%), meaning the models are essentially not learning effectively at repo-level for DeepSeek.\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Are the experimental controls adequate? Is the comparison fair? Are there confounds?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are claims properly supported? Significance testing? Seed variance?\n3. **Experimental coverage**: How complete is the design matrix? Do missing cells weaken claims?\n4. **Result interpretation**: Are conclusions well-supported? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to reproduce?\n\nFormat your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n### Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: Major/Minor)\n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n### Preliminary Score (1-6) and Confidence (1-5)\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017QtP7KP4Av3Dt5Ds1KpGxR","session_id":"dfc6633b-34f1-47e9-a9c0-c3551e8f2a09","uuid":"85aed961-4272-4abd-b71e-69199d57d134","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:51:36.018Z"}
