o-level.\"\n4. **No downstream validation.** Re-repair experiment shows null result (CLS +0.5pp p=0.72). The claimed practical benefit of per-test diagnostics is unsubstantiated.\n5. **Error complementarity contribution is thin.** Oracle ensemble is a ceiling analysis, not a method. No practical ensemble is proposed.\n6. **CWM baseline may be unfairly weak.** 4B/8B LoRA CWM vs the actual 32B Code World Model with mid-training — showing small LoRA fails doesn't indict the approach.\n\n### Missing Related Work\n- AlphaCode/AlphaCode2, CodeRL, APPS benchmark, ReAct/Reflexion\n- Process vs outcome supervision parallel (Lightman et al., Uesato et al.) — CLS vs TRAJ maps directly to this\n- Only ~20 references, lean for a top venue\n\n### R2's Key Argument\nThe paper validates a predictable result (classification < generation for complex outputs) on limited scale, without delivering actionable methods. The contribution is below novelty threshold for a top venue. Would need: complete cross-model validation, include re-repair even if negative, reframe as empirical analysis with tempered claims.\n\n---\n\nNow provide your response to R2's review.\n","uuid":"3afb5d2b-b864-44e3-91d8-363ade398a94","session_id":"dfc6633b-34f1-47e9-a9c0-c3551e8f2a09"}
